            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Adds up how much a tensor CHANGES from one value to the next: the differences between neighbours, made positive, summed into one number.\n\nIt measures the total distance travelled, not how far the values ended up from where they started. A signal that swings a long way and comes back has high energy and no overall change. Use it to ask how much something moved over a stretch of samples - a window of a joint's positions, a recorded gesture - or, with n 2, how jerky the movement was: the total change in the change.\n\n(Despite the name, this is not the physicist's energy or the sum of squares: it is the total variation, the sum of the sizes of the steps.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.energy <dim: int>\nWith no argument the differences run along the LAST axis and there is no\ndim inlet. Give an axis as the argument - t.energy 0 - and a dim box\nappears so it can be changed. Either way the answer is ONE number,\nsummed over everything.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.energy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor). Every input is measured and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nn (int box):\nHow many times to difference before summing. 1 (the default) is total change; 2 is total change in the change. 0 sums the sizes of the values themselves. A tensor not longer than n along the axis gives 0. Negative n is refused with an error. Changing it does not send by itself - the metro in the demo re-sends.\n\ndim (int box, only with an argument):\nThe axis to take the differences along.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA single number, as a one-value tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nBoth messages hold values from 0 to 8, sent five times a second. The steady climb 0 1 2 ... 8 moves 1 at each of its 8 steps: energy 8, the same as the distance from start to finish. The zigzag 0 8 0 8 ... moves 8 at each step: energy 64, although it ends where it began. Same range, same start and end - eight times the travel.\n\nType 2 into n on both: the climb gives 0 (its speed never changes), the zigzag 112 (it reverses with all its speed every step).\n\nRELATED:\nt.diff gives the differences themselves, before summing. np.diff is the NumPy difference. t.length is the size of a tensor, not how much it changes. t.sum adds values without differencing.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds up how much a tensor CHANGES from one value to the next: the differences between neighbours, made positive, summed into one number.\n\nIt measures the total distance travelled, not how far the values ended up from where they started. A signal that swings a long way and comes back has high energy and no overall change. Use it to ask how much something moved over a stretch of samples - a window of a joint's positions, a recorded gesture - or, with n 2, how jerky the movement was: the total change in the change.\n\n(Despite the name, this is not the physicist's energy or the sum of squares: it is the total variation, the sum of the sizes of the steps.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.energy <dim: int>\nWith no argument the differences run along the LAST axis and there is no\ndim inlet. Give an axis as the argument - t.energy 0 - and a dim box\nappears so it can be changed. Either way the answer is ONE number,\nsummed over everything.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.energy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor). Every input is measured and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nn (int box):\nHow many times to difference before summing. 1 (the default) is total change; 2 is total change in the change. 0 sums the sizes of the values themselves. A tensor not longer than n along the axis gives 0. Negative n is refused with an error. Changing it does not send by itself - the metro in the demo re-sends.\n\ndim (int box, only with an argument):\nThe axis to take the differences along.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nA single number, as a one-value tensor.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nBoth messages hold values from 0 to 8, sent five times a second. The steady climb 0 1 2 ... 8 moves 1 at each of its 8 steps: energy 8, the same as the distance from start to finish. The zigzag 0 8 0 8 ... moves 8 at each step: energy 64, although it ends where it began. Same range, same start and end - eight times the travel.\n\nType 2 into n on both: the climb gives 0 (its speed never changes), the zigzag 112 (it reverses with all its speed every step).\n\nRELATED:\nt.diff gives the differences themselves, before summing. np.diff is the NumPy difference. t.length is the size of a tensor, not how much it changes. t.sum adds values without differencing."
        },
